    <body>
      <sec id="article-142674.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Thyroid eye disease (TED) is a chronic, immune-mediated orbitopathy most often associated with Graves disease. The condition arises from autoantibody-mediated inflammation targeting orbital fibroblasts, adipocytes, and extraocular muscles, resulting in a self-perpetuating cycle of cytokine release, tissue expansion, and fibrosis. Clinical manifestations include eyelid retraction, proptosis, diplopia, ocular surface disease, and, in severe cases, dysthyroid optic neuropathy. Disease activity varies from an early inflammatory phase to a stable inactive phase, with severity ranging from mild cosmetic changes to sight-threatening complications. Risk factors include smoking, thyroid dysfunction, age, sex, and genetic predisposition. Conservative measures such as lubrication and risk factor modification suffice for most cases. In contrast, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, targeted biologic therapy, radiotherapy, or surgical decompression are reserved for moderate-to-severe or refractory disease. Long-term outcomes depend on strict maintenance of euthyroid status, early recognition of disease activity, and timely referral for specialized management.</p>
        <p>Through participation in this course, learners develop a structured understanding of TED pathophysiology, risk factors, clinical manifestations, and evidence-based management strategies. The activity emphasizes early detection, accurate disease staging, and application of appropriate therapeutic interventions to improve visual and cosmetic outcomes. Participants learn to implement preventive strategies, interpret severity classifications, and apply multimodal treatment options across disease stages. Collaboration among an interprofessional team, including endocrinologists, ophthalmologists, oculoplastic surgeons, optometrists, pharmacists, and mental health professionals, enhances patient outcomes by aligning systemic and ocular care, addressing comorbidities, optimizing medication use, and supporting psychosocial health. Coordinated care ensures timely interventions for sight-threatening complications and facilitates long-term rehabilitation, enabling patients with TED to maintain visual function and quality of life.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify risk factors, pathophysiologic mechanisms, and early clinical manifestations of thyroid eye disease to facilitate timely diagnosis and referral.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate disease activity from severity using validated scoring systems to guide appropriate therapeutic strategies.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based medical, surgical, and supportive interventions across the spectrum of thyroid eye disease to improve patient outcomes.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with an interprofessional healthcare team, including endocrinology, ophthalmology, oculoplastic surgery, optometry, pharmacy, and mental health, to optimize long-term management and address both visual and psychosocial needs.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=142674&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=142674">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Thyroid eye disease (TED) is a chronic, immune-mediated inflammation of the orbit and is the most common cause of unilateral and bilateral proptosis in adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;About 25% to 50% of Graves disease cases present with thyroid eye disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Goiter-associated&#x000a0;ophthalmopathy was first described in AD 1000 by Avicenna and Al-Jurjani. In 1835, Robert Graves, an Irish physician, described 4 young women with thyrotoxicosis, palpitations, thyroid gland enlargement, and exophthalmos. Graves ophthalmopathy is an orbital inflammation or infiltration involving the periocular soft tissues, resulting in proptosis and ophthalmoplegia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The etiology of TED is incompletely understood; this condition involves a complex, immune-mediated cycle involving the orbital fibroblasts, adipocytes, and lymphocytes. Marked orbital inflammation produces high levels of proinflammatory cytokines, such as interleukin (IL)-1, and prostaglandins that target the orbital fat deposits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Orbital fibroblasts are the primary drivers of the pathogenesis of TED because they have increased expression of thyroid-stimulating hormone receptors and can differentiate into mature adipocytes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;A subpopulation of orbital fibroblasts overexpresses the Thy-1 (CD90) marker.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Transforming growth factor-beta stimulates the Thy-1 fibroblasts to differentiate into adipocytes, while the Thy-1 (+) fibroblasts are stimulated to differentiate into myofibroblasts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;The thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor is the primary target of the autoimmune cascade within the orbit, and serum levels of thyrotropin receptor antibodies correlate with severity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Another possible target molecule is the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor &#x003b3;, which is present on Thy-1(-) fibroblasts and regulates adipogenesis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;The type 1 insulin-like growth factor receptor is another critical autoantigen on Thy-1 (+) fibroblasts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;The stimulation of this receptor enhances the synthesis of IL-16 and the RANTES (regulated upon activation, normal T cell expressed and secreted) complex. This chemokine&#x000a0;recruits CD4+ T cells in the orbital inflammatory cascade.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Thy-1 (-) fibroblasts activated by thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor activation differentiate into adipocytes, secrete IL-6, and recruit B cells and plasma cells. Thy-1 (+) fibroblasts,&#x000a0;activated by the type 1 insulin-like growth factor, secrete IL-16 and RANTES to recruit T cells. T cells engage in CD40-CD154 interactions&#x000a0;that induce IL-1 secretion from fibroblasts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>T cells also produce interferon-&#x003b3; and tumor necrosis factor-alpha to stimulate myofibroblast activity and hyaluronate production. With&#x000a0;IL-6 stimulation, B cells produce more autoantibodies within the orbit. With prolonged inflammation, the Thy-1 (+) cell cluster causes fibrosis of the extraocular muscles, leading to muscle-predominant orbitopathy. In contrast, TED cases with Thy-1 (+) cell clusters exhibit enlargement of the orbital adipose tissue, characterized by fat-predominant orbitopathy.&#x000a0;Individuals younger than 40 have a higher Thy-1 (-) response and show orbital fat expansion. Individuals older than 70 are more likely to exhibit a Thy-1 (+) response, accompanied by fusiform muscle enlargement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The prevalence of thyroid eye disease is approximately 50% among patients with Graves disease. The annual incidence is 16 per 100,000 women and 2.9 per 100,000 men in the United States.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Risk Factors</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ethnicity</bold>: The prevalence is highest in Black individuals, followed by White and Asian persons.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Age</bold>: TED shows a bimodal incidence peak. The incidence is increased in women&#x000a0;aged 40 to 44 and 60 to 64, as well as in men aged 45 to 49 and 65 to 69.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Older patients often have more severe disease and a higher chance of restrictive myopathy and dysthyroid optic neuropathy.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Sex</bold>: There is a higher preponderance in women due to the increased risk of autoimmune diseases in this population. Men tend to have more severe ocular involvement and worse outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r15">[15]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Genetics</bold>: Polymorphisms in genes encoding cytotoxic T-lymphocyte&#x02013;associated antigen 4 (CTLA-4), human leukocyte antigen DRB1 (HLA-DRB1), and tumor necrosis factor-&#x003b1; are associated with TED.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Systemic disease</bold>: Autoimmune disorders like pernicious anemia, systemic lupus erythematosus, Addison disease, vitiligo, celiac disease, and rheumatoid arthritis are associated with a higher risk of TED.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r17">[17]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Environmental factors</bold>: Smoking is strongly associated with an increased severity of TED.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r18">[18]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Dysthyroid status</bold>: At the time of diagnosis, 90% of patients with TED have hyperthyroidism, 6% are euthyroid, 3% have Hashimoto thyroiditis, and 1% have hypothyroidism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Radioactive iodine therapy.&#x000a0;</bold>Treatment with radioactive iodine can cause an&#x000a0;exacerbation in 24% of TED cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Stress</bold>: Psychological stress&#x000a0;can aggravate TED by rebound immune hyperactivity following prolonged corticosteroid-induced immunosuppression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r20">[20]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pregnancy</bold>: New-onset or worsening of TED occurs in 30% of Graves disease cases during the postpartum period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Other factors</bold>: Trauma can be a stimulus for activating an autoimmune cascade in the orbit. High serum cholesterol may also be a risk factor for TED.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r10">[10]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>TED has a self-limited course owing to the absence of orbital lymphoid tissue. This absence&#x000a0;prevents sustained immune activity, resulting in an active phase followed by a stable, quiescent phase. Wilson Rundle categorized the&#x000a0;progression of TED into 3 phases, often referred to as the "Rundle Curve."</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Active or inflammatory phase:</bold> A steep rise in disease severity, lasting 6 months to 5 years (mean 2 years). Smoking can extend this stage.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Static or plateau phase:</bold>&#x000a0;Disease activity persists but does not progress.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Inactive or burnout phase:</bold> TED usually improves after 18 months of disease activity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r22">[22]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>However, the disease rarely completely resolves, and residual fibrotic changes persist in the orbit.&#x000a0;Aggressive immunosuppressive management during the initial active phase can limit the destructive and fibrotic consequences of the immune cascade. During the inactive phase, the disease does not respond to medical management and warrants surgical intervention.</p>
        <p>The cone model is divided into 3 phases and posits that disease progression is due to expansion of the muscle cone against a rigid bony orbit:</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p>Circumferential cone expansion displaces the extraconal fat outward, visible as fat prolapse into the upper and lower eyelids.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Axial elongation of the cone displaces the globe forward, causing proptosis and muscle strain.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cone hypertension and muscle stiffness impede orbital venous drainage, manifesting as orbital venous congestion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r23">[23]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Ocular discomfort is the most common presentation in patients with thyroid eye disease.&#x000a0;Other common symptoms are dryness, tearing, erythema, photophobia, and retro-orbital pain. Visual disturbances, such as blurring and diplopia, require prompt intervention. Furthermore, the onset of TED can precede the diagnosis of GD in 19.9% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Asymmetrical, bilateral TED is also a common presentation. Eyelid retraction is the most common clinical sign of TED, in up to 74% of cases.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Eyelid Changes</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lateral flare:</bold> The altered contour of the lateral upper eyelid is a pathognomonic sign caused by scarring between the lacrimal gland fascia and the levator aponeurosis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Eyelid lag:</bold>&#x000a0;On a vertical downward pursuit,&#x000a0;the excursion of the upper eyelid lags behind the globe.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lagophthalmos: </bold>Incomplete eyelid closure&#x000a0;is a classic sign of TED.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lower eyelid retraction</bold> is present in up to 49% of TED cases.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Upper eyelid retraction</bold> is present in around 90% to 98% of cases. The mechanisms proposed are as follows:&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Enlargement and fibrosis of the levator muscle are the most commonly affected muscles.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scarring between the levator aponeurosis and the surrounding soft tissues.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Overextension of the levator muscle to counteract the ipsilateral tight inferior rectus muscle.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increased sympathetic stimulation of the M&#x000fc;ller muscle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r25">[25]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Ocular Surface Changes (Active TED Phase)</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Following inflammatory damage to the conjunctival mucous glands, the interface between the eyelid and the cornea is destabilized, resulting in tear film alterations.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lacrimal gland inflammation alters the tear film's protein profile and reduces the secretion rate.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent lagophthalmos disturbs the ocular surface,&#x000a0;increases&#x000a0;the reflex tear production rate, and alters the tear osmolarity.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dry eye symptoms and watering&#x000a0;occur&#x000a0;in 13% to 20% of patients.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Superior limbic keratoconjunctivitis&#x000a0;is observed in&#x000a0;0.9% of patients.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent dry eye with exposure keratopathy is observed in 1% to 8% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Corneal ulceration, perforation, and endophthalmitis are present in very severe cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Conjunctival chemosis is more common in moderate to severe cases and is seen in 15% to 25% of patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r27">[27]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Orbital Changes</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypertrophy of the orbital fat and extraocular muscles within the bony orbit raises the intraorbital pressure.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Forward protrusion of the orbit causes proptosis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Constriction of the superior ophthalmic vein by enlarged extraocular muscles results in venous stasis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Venous congestion causes redness and swelling of the eyelids, chemosis, and caruncular edema.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r23">[23]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dependent periorbital puffiness, often in the early morning, is also a sign of impaired venous outflow.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Elevation of the episcleral venous pressure results in elevated intraocular pressure and open-angle glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Venous and lymphatic stasis in the orbit prolongs the half-life of inflammatory cytokines, causing a cycle of inflammation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Spontaneous fracture of the medial or inferior wall of the orbit resulting in spontaneous autodecompression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r28">[28]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Extraocular Muscle Changes</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Inflammation of the extraocular muscle (EOM) occurs during active TED.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infiltration of the EOM occurs through the accumulation of hyaluronate and the development of late-onset fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r29">[29]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The most commonly involved muscles are the inferior rectus, the medial rectus, the superior rectus, the lateral rectus, and the oblique muscles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Restrictive strabismus and loss of binocular single vision.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Diplopia presents initially in 6% to 18% of cases.&#x000a0;Diplopia during the primary and inferior gaze is the most debilitating complication.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fluctuation of diplopia is common, with worsening in the morning and during active disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r29">[29]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Optic Nerve Changes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Dysthyroid Optic Neuropathy (DON) is a potentially reversible optic nerve dysfunction in 4% to 10% of patients with TED<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r2">[2]</xref>. Early symptoms include diminished vision, altered color vision, decreased contrast sensitivity, and constricted visual fields. See<bold> Table 1</bold>. The possible mechanisms are:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Compressive optic neuropathy:</bold>&#x000a0;The most common cause is compression by the enlarged EOM at the orbital apex.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r23">[23]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Optic nerve stretching:</bold>&#x000a0;This occurs in cases of severe fat hypertrophy with proptosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r30">[30]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ischemic optic neuropathy:</bold>&#x000a0;This occurs due to increased resistance to orbital arterial blood flow, leading to diminished perfusion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r31">[31]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Enlargement of the superior ophthalmic vein</bold>&#x000a0;raises retrobulbar pressure and impairs tissue oxygenation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r31">[31]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-142674.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 1.&#x000a0;Eyelid, Facial, and Extraocular Muscle Signs</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 7.40002px;">
                <td style="height: 7.40002px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Eyelid Signs</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1. <bold>Dalrymple sign</bold>: Upper eyelid retraction</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2. <bold>Von Graefe sign</bold>: The upper eyelid lags behind the globe in downgaze</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3. <bold>Vigoroux sign</bold>: Eyelid fullness/swelling</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4. <bold>Grove sign</bold>: Resistance on attempting to pull down the retracted upper eyelid</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5. <bold>Rosenbach sign</bold>: Fine tremors on attempted closure of the eyelids</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6. <bold>Gifford sign</bold>: Difficulty in upper eyelid eversion</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7. <bold>Enroth sign</bold>:&#x000a0; Lower eyelid edema</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8. <bold>Boston sign</bold>: Jerky, irregular movements of the upper eyelid on attempted downgaze</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9. <bold>Kocher sign</bold>: Increased upper eyelid retraction on visual fixation, staring gaze</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10. <bold>Abadie sign</bold>: Levator palpebrae superioris spasm with upper eyelid retraction</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11. <bold>Reesman sign</bold>: Bruit auscultated over the closed eye with a stethoscope</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12. <bold>Snellen Donder sign</bold>: Bruit heard over the open eye after anesthesia, with a stethoscope</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13. <bold>Mean sign</bold>: An increase in the superior scleral show on attempted upward gaze</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Facial Signs</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1. <bold>Stellwag sign</bold>: Incomplete and infrequent blinking</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2. <bold>Joffroy sign</bold>: Absent forehead creases on upward gaze</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3. <bold>Jellinek sign</bold>: Hyperpigmentation of the upper eyelid folds</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4. <bold>Hertoghe sign</bold>: Loss or thinning of the lateral third of the eyebrow</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5. <bold>Sainton sign</bold>: Delayed wrinkling of the forehead on upward gaze</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6. <bold>Pochin sign</bold>: Increased blinking amplitude</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Extraocular Muscle Signs</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1. <bold>M&#x000f6;bius Sign</bold>: Convergence insufficiency</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2. <bold>Sattler sign</bold>: Increased IOP on attempted upward gaze</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3. <bold>Ballet sign</bold>: EOM restriction</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 9px;">
                <td style="height: 9px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.<bold> Jendrassik sign</bold>: Abduction limitation of the orbits</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.<bold> Suker sign</bold>: Poor fixation on attempted abduction</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6. <bold>Wilder sign</bold>: Jerky eye movements on attempted adduction from full abduction</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Pupillary Signs</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1. <bold>Cowen sign:</bold> Jerky constriction to light</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2. <bold>Loewi sign:</bold> Mydriasis with topical adrenaline (1:1000)&#x000a0;</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3. <bold>Knies sign:</bold> Unequal dilation in dim light</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Other Signs</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1. <bold>Goldzeiher sign</bold>: Deep injection of the bulbar conjunctiva</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.<bold> Payne-Trousseau sign</bold>: Globe luxation</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Thyroid eye disease&#x000a0;cases are clinically evaluated based on the disease activity and severity. The activity represents the grade of active inflammation and defines the progression of the disease. The&#x000a0;severity reflects the spectrum of functional and cosmetic deficits.&#x000a0;Numerous classification systems have emerged over the last century to assess and grade the clinical manifestations of TED.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The most well-known system is the NO SPECS classification, which grades the clinical severity based on the mnemonic: No signs or symptoms,&#x000a0;only (mild) signs, soft tissue involvement (eyelids and conjunctiva),&#x000a0;proptosis,&#x000a0;extraocular muscle involvement,&#x000a0;corneal involvement, and&#x000a0;sight loss (optic nerve injury).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;The American Thyroid Association introduced this classification in 1969.&#x000a0;It was updated in 1977 to the modified NO SPECS classification in which each letter was given a numeral 0 through VI.&#x000a0;Each category was then subdivided into different levels of severity<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r33">[33]</xref>. For example, the letter P, standing for proptosis in NO SPECS, was assigned grade III, with three subcategories assigned to different levels of proptosis as shown in&#x000a0;<bold>Table 2&#x000a0;</bold>below.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-142674.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 2. SPECS Classification&#x000a0;</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Class</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Grade</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>&#x000a0;0</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No physical signs or symptoms</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>I</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Only signs</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>II</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Soft tissue involvement</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>&#x000a0;II-0</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Absent</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>&#x000a0;II-1</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Minimal</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>&#x000a0;II-2</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Moderate</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>&#x000a0;II-3</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Marked</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>III&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Proptosis (&#x0003e;2 mm above the normal upper limits)</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>III-1&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 to 4 mm</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>III-2&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 to 7 mm</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>III-3&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;8 mm&#x000a0;</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>IV&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Extraocular muscle involvement (usually with diplopia)</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>IV-0&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Absent</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>IV-1&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Limitation of motion at the extremes of gaze</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>IV-2&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Evident restriction of movements</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>IV-3&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fixed globe/globes</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>V&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Corneal involvement (due to lagophthalmos)</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>V-0&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Absent</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>V-1&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Stippling of the cornea</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>V-2&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ulceration</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>V-3&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Clouding, necrosis, and perforation</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>VI</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Sight loss (optic nerve involvement)</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>VI-0&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Absent</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>&#x000a0;VI-1</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Disc pallor or visual field defect, vision 20/20 to 20/60</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>VI-2&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Disc pallor or visual field defect, vision 20/70 to 20/200</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 41.25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>VI-3&#x000a0;</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 560.75px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Blindness, vision less than 20/200</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Notably, the NO SPECS system only grades the clinical severity of TED and does not assess the disease activity.&#x000a0; In 1981, Van Dyk proposed the RELIEF variation of the NO SPECS classification (see <bold>Table 3</bold>).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Because these classification systems dictate the treatment strategies without considering the status of active inflammation, they are now obsolete.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-142674.table2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 3.&#x000a0;RELIEF Variation of SPECS Classification</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 366.8px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">R - Resistance to retropulsion</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 366.8px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">E - Edema of the conjunctiva and caruncle</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 366.8px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">L - Lacrimal gland enlargement</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 366.8px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">I - Injection over horizontal rectus muscle insertions</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 366.8px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">E - Edema of the eyelids</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 366.8px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">F - Fullness of the eyelids</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>A new system, the&#x000a0;<bold>Clinical Activity Score (CAS),</bold> was proposed in 1989, considering the classic signs of inflammation, pain, swelling, redness, and impaired function (see<bold> Table 4</bold>). The European Group on Graves Orbitopathy (EUGOGO) later amended the CAS system.&#x000a0;According to the amended CAS, active TED scores greater than 3 out of 7 points at the first visit or greater than 4 out of 10 points at subsequent visits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-142674.table3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 4.&#x000a0;Clinical Activity Score</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="width: 734.8px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>For initial CAS, score items 1-7 only</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 84px;">
                <td style="width: 734.8px; height: 84px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="order"><list-item><p>Spontaneous orbital pain</p></list-item><list-item><p>Gaze-evoked orbital pain</p></list-item><list-item><p>Eyelid edema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Eyelid erythema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Conjunctival congestion (involving &#x0003e;1 quadrant)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Chemosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Caruncular edema</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="width: 734.8px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>At the follow-up visit (1-3 months), a score out of 10 (including items 8-10)</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13.8572px;">
                <td style="width: 734.8px; height: 13.8572px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>8. Increase of &#x0003e;2 mm in proptosis</p>
<p>9. Diminished uniocular excursion in any direction of &#x0003e;8&#x000b0;</p>
<p>10. Decrease of visual acuity equivalent to &#x0003e;1 Snellen line</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>The EUGOGO&#x000a0;proposed a severity assessment protocol to categorize the severity of TED into mild, moderate-to-severe (see<bold> Table 5</bold>), and sight-threatening categories&#x000a0;as&#x000a0;summarized below:</p>
        <p>
<bold>Soft Tissue Assessment (Pain Not Included)</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Eyelid swelling</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Absent</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mild: None of the features defining moderate or severe categories</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Moderate: Definite swelling, no lower eyelid festoons or angulation of the upper eyelid skin fold in downgaze</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe: Lower eyelid festoons or upper eyelid fold becomes rounded at 45&#x000b0; downgaze</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Eyelid erythema</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Absent</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Present</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Conjunctival redness</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Absent</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mild: equivocal or minimal&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Moderate: &#x0003c;50% of definite conjunctival involvement</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe: &#x0003e;50% of definite conjunctival involvement</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Conjunctival edema</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Absent</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Present: Separation of conjunctiva from sclera present in more than one-third of the total height of the palpebral aperture, or the conjunctiva prolapses anterior to the gray line of the eyelid</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Inflammation of the caruncle or plica semilunaris</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Absent</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Present: Plica prolapses through the closed eyelids, or inflammation of the caruncle or plica</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Eyelid&#x000a0;margin assessment (at the midpupillary line)</bold></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Palpebral aperture&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Upper/lower eyelid retraction&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Levator function&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lagophthalmos</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bell phenomenon</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Proptosis Assessment: </bold>Using Hertel exophthalmometry&#x000a0;to record the distance from the lateral orbital rim to the corneal apex</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ocular Motility Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The prism cover test</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monocular ductions</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Head posture</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Torsion</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Binocular single vision</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Corneal Integrity Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Normal</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Punctate keratopathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ulcer</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Perforation</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Optic Nerve Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual acuity (logMAR or Snellen)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Afferent pupil defect (present/absent)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Color vision</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual field analysis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optic disc assessment (normal/atrophy/edema)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-142674.table4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 5. EUGOGO Severity Classification</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 98px;">
                <td style="width: 125.312px; height: 98px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Mild</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 420.973px; height: 98px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>One or more of the following:&#x000a0;</p>
<list list-type="order"><list-item><p>Minor eyelid retraction &#x0003c;2mm</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mild soft-tissue involvement&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Exophthalmos &#x0003c;3mm above the reference range&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Transient or no diplopia</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 98px;">
                <td style="width: 125.312px; height: 98px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Moderate-to-Severe</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 420.973px; height: 98px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>One or more of the following:&#x000a0;</p>
<list list-type="order"><list-item><p>Eyelid retraction &#x0003e;2mm</p></list-item><list-item><p>Moderate-to-severe soft-tissue involvement&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Exophthalmos &#x0003e;3mm above the reference range&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inconstant or constant diplopia</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13.7144px;">
                <td style="width: 125.312px; height: 13.7144px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Sight-Threatening</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 420.973px; height: 13.7144px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dysthyroid optic neuropathy or corneal breakdown</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>A novel classification, the&#x000a0;<bold>VISA </bold>system, was proposed in 2006 with four parameters: vision, inflammation/congestion, strabismus/motility restriction, and appearance/exposure. The International Thyroid Eye Disease Society defined separate parameters for the assessment at the first and subsequent visits in 2018. An&#x000a0;<bold>inflammation (I) score </bold>greater than&#x000a0;4 of 10 points indicates&#x000a0;inactive disease and should be managed conservatively. A score of greater than 5 out of 10 points with evidence of progression of inflammation should be managed aggressively. The clinical assessment and scoring are similar to CAS-EUGOGO recommendations except for the following changes:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Chemosis</bold>:+1 point if the conjunctiva lies behind the gray line of the eyelid, +2 points if the conjunctiva extends anterior to the gray line</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Eyelid edema</bold>:+1 point if no overhanging of tissues, +2 points if upper eyelid skin folds or lower eyelid festoons are present</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Strabismus/motility restriction</bold>: Ductions measured to the nearest 5&#x000b0; in all 4 gazes, using the corneal reflex method. Any change in the ductions of &#x0003e;12&#x000b0; in any direction suggests progression.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Clinical assessment protocols given by the VISA and EUGOGO teams are not interchangeable. Hence, only a single system should be used for a specific patient.&#x000a0;All the scoring systems focus on a qualitative assessment, assigning equal weight to all symptoms, regardless of their diagnostic significance. The equality of symptoms could potentially undermine the accurate monitoring of disease progression. For example, the cut-off points (3/7 or 4/10 in CAS scoring) may not accurately reflect the extent of the disease.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ocular Imaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intraocular vasculature can be imaged using optical coherence tomography angiography (OCTA). OCTA analysis of the macula and the optic nerve head, which shows perfusion changes within the eye, corresponds to disease activity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;Choroidal imaging is also performed through enhanced depth imaging, high-definition optical coherence tomography, and swept-source optical coherence tomography of the subfoveal choroid. Subfoveal choroidal thickness changes are well-documented with progression of TED, with changes in the choroidal vascularity index corresponding to the severity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;The clinical role of these imaging modalities remains debatable, but early studies indicate a promising new trend in assessing intraocular vasculature in TED.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Orbital Imaging</bold>
<bold>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Orbital imaging provides valuable insight into the diagnosis and management of TED.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ultrasound Orbit</bold>
</p>
        <p>Ultrasonography of the orbit&#x000a0;can measure the EOM thickness.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Computed Tomography Scan</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Characteristic fusiform enlargement of EOM is an</bold>&#x000a0;enlarged muscle belly with relative sparing of tendons. Long-standing cases show significant EOM changes referred to as the "Coca-Cola bottle sign." Orbital imaging in unilateral TED cases confirms asymmetric bilateral EOM changes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;<bold>The Barrett index</bold> is useful for quantitatively measuring EOM thickness and apical crowding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;The vertical index is the sum of vertical muscle diameters divided by the height of the orbit. The horizontal index is calculated similarly. The greater of the 2 ratios is the Barrett index. A Barrett muscle index greater than 67% predicts optic neuropathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Imaging of dysthryoid optic neuropathy&#x000a0;</bold>may show soft tissue signs like apical crowding, enlarged superior ophthalmic vein,&#x000a0;enlarged EOM (especially the medial rectus), Barrett muscle index greater than 67%, orbital fat prolapse across the superior orbital fissure, perineural fat effacement,&#x000a0;and enlarged lacrimal gland.&#x000a0;The bony orbit signs may demonstrate an increased angle of the orbital apex and a greater angle of the medial wall. Coronal CT scan of orbital apex shows a narrow, clear ring of low density around the optic nerve due to bulging adipose tissue. Stretch neuropathy is diagnosed by a "taut nerve" in cases of severe proptosis.&#x000a0;<bold>Classification of orbitopathy includes&#x000a0;</bold>type 1 (lipogenic), type 2 (myogenic), and type 3 (mixed). Accurate coronal images aid in the detection of soft tissue remodeling of EOM and fat enlargement.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>CT exophthalmometry</bold> is the gold standard because it is accurate, reproducible, and unaffected by periorbital tissue edema. The interzygomatic line connects the ventral zygomatic borders bilaterally in axial view.&#x000a0;The various techniques using the interzygomatic line are:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The perpendicular distance between the anterior surface of the cornea and the interzygomatic line corresponds to the Hertel exophthalmometry value.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r40">[40]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The distance between the interzygomatic line and the posterior sclera (normal value = 9.9&#x000a0;&#x000b1; 1.9 mm).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r41">[41]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The observation of less than one-third of the globe lying behind the interzygomatic line.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r40">[40]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>This technique&#x000a0;is not feasible in patients who have undergone lateral wall decompression surgery. A novel method uses the posterior clinoid process as a landmark and measures the distance between it and the anterior corneal surface.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r42">[42]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Magnetic Resonance Imaging Scan</bold>
</p>
        <p>MRI is an excellent modality for assessing disease activity. MRI parameters, such as the T2 signal-intensity ratio, the T1 signal-intensity ratio following gadolinium administration, and the apparent diffusion coefficient, correlate with the CAS severity scale. In T1-weighted images, the EOMs appear isointense to facial muscles in inactive TED,&#x000a0;but enhance with&#x000a0;gadolinium contrast.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;In T2-weighted images, EOMs appear hyperintense during active TED and hypointense during inactive TED.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r37">[37]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Orbital Color Doppler Imaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>This technique assesses the hemodynamic changes in the orbit following an increase in orbital volume. The primary vessels imaged are the ophthalmic artery, central retinal artery, and superior ophthalmic vein. The peak systolic velocity, end-diastolic velocity, and resistive index are important indicators of ocular perfusion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;TED demonstrates orbits with an initial increase in arterial blood flow during the inflammatory active phase, followed by a significant reduction in orbital blood flow during the inactive phase.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;A sign of substantial loss in orbital arterial flow velocities occurs in vision-threatening TED and dysthyroid optic neuropathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Prevention</bold>
</p>
        <p>Clinical assessment and accurate grading of thyroid eye disease are essential for appropriate management.&#x000a0;Primary prevention includes smoking cessation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r45">[45]</xref>&#x000a0;Secondary prevention includes early detection of any dysthyroid state and prompt management to prevent disease progression;&#x000a0;tertiary prevention includes using artificial tears to help prevent exposure keratopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;Cosmetic and visual rehabilitation are essential. Medical management includes the prompt restoration of euthyroid status, which is essential.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Mild TED</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients with corneal exposure and ocular surface symptoms require extensive lubrication with artificial tears, gels, and ointments. Overnight eyelid taping, occlusive eye pads, cold compresses, or sleeping with the head elevated are of questionable benefit. However, selenium supplements can improve the quality of life, reduce inflammation, and slow the progression of TED.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r48">[48]</xref></p>
        <p>Eyelid retraction &#x0003c;2 mm is managed by:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Transconjunctival injection: 5 units of botulinum toxin type A suspension</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Transconjunctival injection: 10 mg of triamcinolone&#x000a0;suspension</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Both techniques demonstrate excellent results for the temporary resolution of eyelid retraction.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Moderate-to-Severe TED</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Active cases&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Early immunosuppression is essential, and corticosteroids are&#x000a0;the first-line treatment. The EUGOGO management protocol consists of low-dose pulse therapy with intravenous methylprednisolone (IVMP), 500 mg weekly for the initial 6 weeks.&#x000a0;Over the next 6 weeks, the dose is tapered to 250 mg weekly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r49">[49]</xref>&#x000a0;Severe active cases require prolonged oral corticosteroids for 6 months.&#x000a0;Withdrawal of steroids may worsen the disease, but this can be prevented by adjuvant orbital radiotherapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r50">[50]</xref></p>
        <p>Immunomodulators&#x000a0;are considered<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>the&#x000a0;second-line treatment. Indications for their use include steroid intolerance, steroid dependence, disease refractory to steroids, or contraindications to steroid use. Furthermore, methotrexate 7.5&#x000a0;to 10 mg weekly has shown a significant improvement in CAS and ocular motility within 12 months of treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;Azathioprine can be combined with low-dose orbital radiation or IVMP for moderate-to-severe TED<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r52">[52]</xref>.&#x000a0;Mycophenolate mofetil therapy demonstrated a superior CAS response with minimal disease reactivation compared to other treatment options.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r53">[53]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Immunobiologic agents</bold>
</p>
        <p>Rituximab, a humanized chimeric monoclonal antibody targeting CD20 on B cells, has shown a 100% response rate and minimal reactivation in patients with moderate-to-severe TED.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;Tocilizumab (IL-6 receptor antibody), adalimumab (tumor necrosis factor[TNF]-&#x003b1; antibody), and infliximab (TNF-&#x003b1; antibody) also show promising results. Teprotumumab, a specific type 1 insulin-like growth factor receptor blocker, is administered as 8 infusions over 24 weeks. The improvement in CAS, proptosis, diplopia, and quality of life, with a reduction in proptosis, is comparable to that achieved with surgical orbital decompression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r55">[55]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Inactive cases</bold>
</p>
        <p>For cases with lagophthalmos, dry eye disease symptoms, and exposure keratopathy, adequate lubrication and corneal protection are advised. Appropriate surgical management is effective for residual proptosis, strabismus, eyelid retraction, and significant lagophthalmos. Surgical decompression in patients with euthyroid status and inactive disease provides sustained and predictable cosmetic results, with improvement in orbital perfusion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r56">[56]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Sight-threatening disease</bold>
</p>
        <p>High-dose systemic corticosteroid, 1 g IVMP daily for 3 days, is the most effective therapy for DON cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r57">[57]</xref>&#x000a0;Orbital radiotherapy is the second-line treatment that reduces the need for surgical orbital decompression.&#x000a0;Urgent orbital decompression is reserved for active TED with DON, not responding to high-dose IVMP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r58">[58]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical options</bold>
</p>
        <p>Elective surgical options are reserved for stable inactive thyroid eye disease cases not responding to conservative or maximal medical management. During cosmetic rehabilitation,&#x000a0;orbital decompression surgery is the primary procedure to correct proptosis. After 6 months, strabismus surgery is performed to achieve adequate ocular alignment.&#x000a0;After an additional 6 months, a definitive eyelid retraction surgery helps restore the cosmetic and functional status.&#x000a0;Indications for orbital decompression are corneal exposure, DON, disfiguring proptosis, chronic pain or discomfort, and congestion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r59">[59]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis may include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Nonspecific orbital inflammatory disease:</bold> Bilateral proptosis with lacrimal gland enlargement
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>EOM enlargement involves the muscle belly and the tendinous origin. The lacrimal gland enlarges and prolapses out of the lacrimal fossa. This disease can be associated with systemic autoimmune diseases, such as systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, polymyositis, dermatomyositis, and IgG4-related disease. Serology and soft-tissue biopsy are diagnostic.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lymphoma:</bold> Bilateral proptosis with associated lymphadenopathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Results of complete blood counts are usually normal. Orbital CT scan shows bilateral diffuse soft-tissue enlargement with erosion of the bony orbit. Histopathology and immunohistochemistry features on the soft tissue biopsy are diagnostic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r60">[60]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Orbital floor fracture:</bold> Altered globe position and limitation of EOM movements
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A history of trauma is followed by enophthalmos and restriction of upgaze. An orbital CT scan shows the bony defect with entrapped soft tissues.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Amyloidosis:</bold> Bilateral proptosis with associated neuropathy
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Soft-tissue biopsy reveals eosinophilic material on histopathology, with birefringence on polarized light microscopy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r61">[61]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Approximately 80% of thyroid eye disease cases require conservative management with topical lubricant eye drops.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Approximately 5% of TED cases require systemic corticosteroids or immunomodulatory therapy. About 15% of patients undergo surgical intervention. Long-term follow-up and close monitoring for disease complications are essential.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Thyroid eye disease cases can present with the following complications:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Dysthyroid optic neuropathy (compressive or ischemic)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optic atrophy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lagophthalmos, exposure keratopathy, or keratomalacia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Open-angle glaucoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Restrictive strabismus with diplopia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent proptosis and eyelid retraction causing cosmetic disfigurement</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Orbital venous stasis and venous occlusion</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s12" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Cosmetic&#x000a0;treatment is a crucial component of TED management. Common cosmetic concerns are proptosis, strabismus, upper and lower eyelid retraction, lateral flare, and lagophthalmos. Asymmetrical proptosis is common during inactive disease. A graded unilateral or bilateral surgical orbital decompression helps achieve a cosmetically symmetrical look.&#x000a0;The surgical plan is customized based on the degree of proptosis correction, the pattern of strabismus, and the orbital anatomy.</p>
        <p>
<bold>&#x000a0;Surgical approaches are individualized:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lateral wall decompression</bold>&#x000a0;via Kronlein, modified Kronlein, Stallard-Wright, Berke, and lateral eyelid crease incisions
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The preferred technique is ab interno bone removal with sparing of the lateral orbital rim.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r62">[62]</xref>&#x000a0;Complications include intraoperative cerebrospinal leak, infraorbital anesthesia, and new-onset diplopia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r63">[63]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Medial wall decompression</bold>&#x000a0;using transcutaneous, transconjunctival, or transnasal approaches
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The transnasal endoscopic approach is ideal for surgeries on the medial wall.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r64">[64]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Floor decompression</bold>&#x000a0;using the transorbital approach by transcutaneous or transconjunctival incisions</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Two-wall decompression </bold>using combined medial wall and floor removal&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r65">[65]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Combined lateral wall and floor decompression</bold>&#x000a0;with the transconjunctival or transcanthal swinging eyelid crease approach&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r66">[66]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Three walls</bold>, including the floor, lateral wall, and medial wall, spare the orbital roof&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Four-wall decompression</bold>, including the floor, medial wall, lateral wall, and posterolateral roof&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r67">[67]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Fat decompression</bold> in fat-predominant orbitopathy&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r68">[68]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Balanced decompression </bold>with the medial and lateral wall&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r69">[69]</xref>&#x000a0;
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The rate of new-onset strabismus is lower with a balanced decompression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r70">[70]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Surgical orbital decompression corrects the proptosis by 2&#x000a0;to 2.5 mm per wall.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r71">[71]</xref>&#x000a0;Orbital fat is the fifth wall of the orbit that can be used for surgical decompression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-142674.r69">[69]</xref>&#x000a0;Strabismus surgery is planned as a second-stage intervention, after the inflammation from the orbital surgery has subsided, and the tissue remodeling has stabilised. The restricted EOM is recessed to achieve orthophoria in primary gaze. The final correction for the eyelid retraction should be performed at least 3 to 6 months after the strabismus surgery. Blepharomyotomy, levator recession, and spacer grafts are common strategies for eyelid retraction correction.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Patients with TED present to various care settings, from primary care to specialty clinics. These patients&#x000a0;require thorough evaluation&#x000a0;for&#x000a0;ocular and orbital manifestations, thyroid status, and cardiovascular risk, with prompt initiation of thyroid hormonal treatment. TED management requires an&#x000a0;interdisciplinary team that includes an ophthalmologist, an oculofacial plastic surgeon, an endocrinologist, an optometrist, a primary care clinician, and pharmacists. Timely referrals are crucial for optimizing visual and cosmetic outcomes.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>All patients with thyroid disorders should receive routine screening for early signs of thyroid eye disease. Regular follow-up of asymptomatic Graves disease and mild TED cases helps facilitate prompt diagnosis and management of sight-threatening complications. Clinicians should inform patients of the early signs of DON, which include altered color vision, diminished contrast sensitivity, visual field loss, and decreased visual acuity (loss of peripheral vision, as well as faded, dim, and blurred vision).</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s15" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Patients with classic symptoms of thyroid eye disease, but no history of thyroid disorders or who are euthyroid at presentation, require an endocrine evaluation and orbital imaging, which are essential for a definitive diagnosis in the absence of a typical clinical presentation.&#x000a0;The management includes strict maintenance of euthyroid status, cessation of smoking, adequate lubrication, control of intraocular pressure, and monitoring of disease progression according to the CAS/VISA scoring systems at regular follow-up. Any sign of progression to active disease or sight-threatening disease warrants urgent management.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>TED cases require an interdisciplinary effort for early diagnosis and timely management. Clinicians and endocrinologists are the primary contacts for thyroid disorder cases. All predisposed patients should undergo regular eye evaluations. Long-term&#x000a0;maintenance of euthyroid status is the foremost pillar for TED prevention. Smoking cessation and treatment of nicotine dependence are essential, with involvement of psychiatrists, addiction clinicians, counseling, and patient education. Sight-threatening disease and cosmetic disfigurement are also sources of anxiety for the patients.&#x000a0;Mental health clinicians and social workers are essential for addressing psychosocial issues.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Ophthalmologists and oculoplastic personnel are essential in the long-term follow-up, medical, and surgical management of TED cases. Visual and cosmetic rehabilitation are critical endpoints of TED management. Ophthalmologists and optometrists&#x000a0;manage diplopia resulting from uncorrected or residual strabismus with prism correction,&#x000a0;and ocular surface disease through various levels of dry eye therapy&#x000a0;and surface protection (scleral lenses).&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-142674.s17">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=142674&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=142674">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://mdsearchlight.com/eye-health/thyroid-eye-disease/?utm_source=pubmedlink&#x00026;utm_campaign=MDS&#x00026;utm_content=142674">Click here for a simplified version.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/articlelibrary/commentarticle/142674/?utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=comments&#x00026;utm_content=142674">Comment on this article.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
